  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Body, Capital, and Screens shows clear early readership through 1,956 OAPEN downloads, while its citation record remains at 0 in OpenAlex. The book is openly accessible in repository networks, has library holdings recorded in WorldCat and Open Library, and the available evidence points to practical visibility rather than established scholarly citation impact.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published by Amsterdam University Press in 2020, Body, Capital, and Screens sits at the intersection of film and media studies, social and cultural history, and the history of the body. The volume’s subject matter is reflected in the metadata signals attached to it, with concepts pointing toward architecture, art, and visual arts, and its abstract describing a collection focused on how visual media shaped understandings of health and the healthy self in the twentieth century.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The strongest impact signal in the record is usage: the book has accumulated 1,956 downloads through OAPEN, indicating sustained reader access. The monthly pattern suggests ongoing consultation rather than a single burst of interest, which is consistent with a work being used as a reference point or teaching resource in an open-access environment.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The dissemination record is also notable. OpenAIRE identifies multiple repository instances for the book, including open copies, showing that the title has been distributed across repository infrastructure. In addition, library discovery signals are present through WorldCat and Open Library holdings, which support findability and institutional access.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">At the same time, the citation record is still minimal: OpenAlex reports 0 citations. That does not diminish the book’s relevance, but it does mean that the current evidence is better read as audience reach and availability than as documented scholarly uptake. No event record is available in the archive, so public-discussion evidence is not currently part of the story.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, the record portrays a book with demonstrable readership and broad open distribution, especially through repository and library channels, while its formal citation footprint remains too early or too thin to support conclusions about longer-term scholarly influence.</p>",
